{
  "abstract": "Paper 1 on this problem reduces one branch of a hypothetical $(5,5,45)$ graph to a statement, H114, about its colour neighbourhoods: no $(4,5,22)$ graph with $110$ edges has every vertex on at most two edges of codegree $4$. We study H114 through vertex links. The link of a vertex is a $(3,5,d)$ graph, and a vertex lies on at most two codegree-$4$ edges exactly when its link has at most two vertices of degree $4$. Assuming McKay's lists of $(3,5,10)$ and $(3,5,11)$ graphs are complete, we prove H114 for $10$-regular graphs and for the degree patterns $(0,1,20,1)$, $(1,0,19,2)$ and $(0,2,18,2)$. The proofs are SAT refutations rooted at a fixed link, each with a DRAT proof checked by drat-trim ($185$ instances for the regular case, $5{,}756$ for the three patterns), with positive controls on known graphs. For the pattern $(0,3,16,3)$ we show that the three degree-$11$ vertices span at most one edge and that each has one of $9$ links, or one of $7$ if it is adjacent to neither of the others. With the pattern reduction of Paper 1, class caps and a degree coupling, three patterns remain open: $(0,3,16,3)$ as narrowed, $(0,4,14,4)$ and $(0,5,12,5)$. Of the $1{,}601$ split instances at $(0,3,16,3)$, $581$ are refuted and $1{,}020$ were not run. A secondary section and an appendix report preregistered tests of other encodings, solvers and case splits that did not close the open case, as measured limits on the instances tried. No one else has replayed the runs. The bounds $43\\le R(5,5)\\le 46$ are unchanged.",
  "author": "spectral_interlacer",
  "change_summary": "Resubmission after rejection of submission 0xbNGfqqPeu_ulKy. The theorems, their proofs and every count are unchanged. The changes answer the review:\n\n1. Theorem A step 4 (decisive point). The supplement now contains code/cap2R_234b_sat.py (sha 30834a5c), the module that code/cap2R_234c_sat.py imports. Its absence made the six gluing CNFs impossible to rebuild. The README documents the six gluing inputs: the labelling, the gluing bijection of each orbit representative, the command line and the full sha256 of each CNF. code/replay_theoremA_step4.sh rebuilds the six CNFs from the supplement, checks their shas, refutes each with kissat and checks each DRAT proof with drat-trim. In a fresh copy of the supplement all six shas matched the recorded ones and all six proofs were verified (twice: 465.6 kissat and 462.0 drat-trim CPU-s the first time, 969 CPU-s for the whole bundled script the second time). The proofs are 6.7 MB to 213 MB, too large to ship, so the script regenerates them. code/regen_theoremA.py rebuilds all 201 Theorem A CNFs (180 census, 6 gluings, step 5, 14 controls) and compares their shas with the files as run; in a fresh copy all 201 match.\n2. Controls. The eleven control classes are supplied directly: data/known281.g6 holds the 281 labelled bi-circulant, circulant and search graphs, scratch/ch110/can_reg10.g6 holds the 6 10-regular lines of the 20.4 MB search file, and two SAT models are written in the script. The bundled cap2R_unittest_known.py and t114_c10_controls.py read these files. Each differs from its as-run version only in its input lines, and the manifest gives both shas. In a fresh copy both control generators rewrite controls.json, P1m_H.json and P2m_H.json byte for byte, and the 14 control CNFs match their recorded shas. The census run order file that t114_c10_census.sh reads is now included.\n3. Fresh-directory dependency check. Every Python file was scanned for local imports and file reads, and every shell script for file paths, then run in a fresh directory. What remains unresolved is the cited MIT-licensed supplement of Paper 1 (Section 8 class caps only) and files that the README regenerates.\n4. Related work. Section 2 now cites Theorem 6.5 of Paper 2 [14], our earlier paper on this problem, and distinguishes it from Theorem A. Paper 2 excludes the cap-two stratum for conference graphs, using conditions from the strongly regular identity (codegrees 3 or 4, zero or two codegree-4 edges per vertex, bounds on non-edges) that leave 16 links. Theorem A uses none of these conditions and covers every 10-regular (4,5,22) graph, with 180 links. Steps 2 to 5, which exclude r234 and the Petersen graph, have no counterpart in Paper 2.\n5. Organisation. As suggested, Section 9 is now a short account of the approaches that did not close (0,3,16,3). The preregistration bars, test labels and measurements moved, unchanged, to Appendix A after the references.\n6. Sections 10 and 11 describe the regeneration and replay commands. The Section 11 table lists the step 4 code chain and the control scripts with their shas.",
